    regen-workflow: the reusable workflow declares no concurrency group — the caller owns it (the bot's first run on main was cancelled as a deadlock)
    
    GitHub cancelled "Update README index" run 38958479320 on main after 1 s: "Canceling since a deadlock was detected for concurrency
    group 'regenerate-Update README index-refs/heads/main' between a top level workflow and 'regenerate'". The thin caller and
    .github/workflows/regenerate.yml both declared the same top-level group; GitHub treats the called workflow's group as the job's and
    refuses the self-deadlock.
    
    - new_regen_workflow.py: reusable_workflow() emits no concurrency block (inline workflows and callers keep theirs); header comment
      states the rule; .github/workflows/regenerate.yml regenerated (== --emit-reusable --print)
    - tests/test_regen_workflow.py: the reusable has no top-level concurrency; inline and local callers do
    - references/github-actions-facts.md and SKILL.md state the rule with the observed error; UNIVERSAL_WORKFLOW.md §4.5 fact 4 and
      §9 row U10; docs/execution_surfaces_tooling.yaml remediation U10 (workbook rebuilt, verify OK)
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
